  "outcome": "NO_DIFFERENCE_DETECTED",
  "reading": "On 1,634 D. melanogaster embryo distal elements accessible in clusters of exactly one of 14 tissues (Calderon et al. 2022, 0-20 h, the source's own marker call) and lying outside the body of the gene the boundary rule names, the gene with the nearest 5' end inside the element's domain, delimited by 3,121 Hi-C boundaries of nuclear-cycle-14 embryos (Stadler et al. 2017, the authors' manual call), is expressed at 5 counts per million or more and highest in the element's own tissue, in RNA pooled over that tissue's cells, for 88 of 1,634 (5.4%), against 84 of 1,634 (5.1%) for the gene with the nearest 5' end on the arm; the rules differ on 234 of 1,634 elements; difference +0.24 points, 95% -0.34 to +0.95 over 1-Mb clusters. No difference detected between the boundary rule and nearest TSS. With 234 elements differing, a difference smaller than about 2.6 points could go undetected. For 115 of the 234 elements where the rules differ, the nearest-TSS gene contains the element, which can favour nearest TSS. Against the gene-structure rule on the same elements (148 of 1,634, 9.1%; the two differ on 943, and the gene-structure rule names the host for 717), the difference is -3.67 points, 95% -5.96 to -1.37: the gene-structure rule is ahead of the boundary rule. The boundary rule's genes match the element's tissue more often than genes at a matched distance (4.4%, one-sided p 0.001), so position carries tissue information here. With the same boundaries rotated along each arm the rule averages 5.5% on these elements (one-sided p 0.612), so the measured positions are not shown to carry more than the boundaries' number and spacing. Between genes (beside, deciding nothing) the boundary rule names a different gene from nearest TSS for only 90 of 917 elements, fewer than the stop rule's 200, so this boundary set cannot decide the between-gene question: there it is concordant for 64 (7.0%) against 63 (6.9%) for nearest TSS, +0.11 points [-0.46, +0.70]. On all 2,518 distal elements (beside, deciding nothing; the rules differ on 273) the boundary rule is concordant for 238 (9.5%) against 231 (9.2%) for nearest TSS, +0.28 points [-0.22, +0.82], and against 299 (11.9%) for the gene-structure rule, -2.42 points [-3.85, -0.94]. Accessibility is not function, and a boundary is not a regulatory unit by itself: this says the attributed gene is expressed where the element is open, not that the element acts on it. The boundaries come from 2-3 h embryos, the marker calls mostly from 6-16 h, and accessibility and RNA from different nuclei.",
